h2. 4. Functional Design

h3. 4.1 Payment Initiation (PIS)
	•	Clients call JPM Global PIS APIs
	•	Kratos creates a payment using Tink native PIS APIs
	•	Tink handles:
	•	Bank discovery
	•	PSU authentication (SCA)
	•	Payment execution
	•	Tink returns payment status SENT

Ownership:
	•	Tink – payment initiation
	•	JPM – lifecycle management and abstraction

⸻

h3. 4.2 Settlement & Reconciliation
	•	Settlement is not determined by Tink
	•	Kratos queries JPM Treasury Transaction Details APIs
	•	Settlement confirmed when funds arrive in JPM account
	•	Status transitions:
	•	CREATED → SENT → SETTLED / FAILED

Ownership:
	•	JPM Treasury – settlement truth
	•	Kratos – reconciliation logic

⸻

h3. 4.3 Payment Status & Events
	•	Tink webhooks notify payment initiation outcomes
	•	Treasury events confirm settlement
	•	Kratos exposes:
	•	GET /payment/{id}
	•	Client webhooks

JPM is the system of record for payment status.

⸻

h3. 4.4 Refunds (Closed-Loop)
	•	Refund requests initiated via JPM APIs
	•	Kratos validates original PIS payment
	•	Refunds executed via JPM Treasury rails
	•	Supports:
	•	Full refunds
	•	Partial refunds

Auto-refunds are out of scope.

⸻

h3. 4.5 UI, Consent & Redirects
	•	JPM hosts:
	•	Consent screens
	•	Terms & Conditions
	•	Success / Failure pages
	•	TinkLink used only for bank redirection and SCA
	•	Kratos controls dynamic redirect URLs

⸻

h2. 5. Analytics, Reporting & Billing
	•	Kratos emits:
	•	API usage metrics
	•	Payment success / failure rates
	•	Settlement latency
	•	Billing triggered on:
	•	Successful payment initiation
	•	Refund initiation
	•	Regulatory reporting (PSD2) handled via JPM pipelines

⸻

h2. 6. Migration Strategy

h3. 6.1 Phase 1 – Foundation
	•	Enable Tink Oxford multi-tenant
	•	Implement PISP V2
	•	Treasury-based settlement confirmation
	•	Begin V1 client migration

⸻

h3. 6.2 Phase 2 – De-customisation
	•	Decommission Jersey single-tenant
	•	Remove bespoke Tink components
	•	Migrate AIS dependencies

⸻

h3. 6.3 Phase 3 – Optimisation
	•	Improve reporting and analytics
	•	UX improvements
	•	Prepare for VRP / sVRP expansion

⸻

h2. 7. Non-Functional Requirements

|| Category || Requirement ||
| Availability | ≥ 99.9% |
| Scalability | Multi-tenant capable |
| Security | JPM internal approvals |
| Performance | Defined API & UI SLAs |
| Observability | End-to-end auditability |

⸻

h2. 8. Risks & Mitigations

|| Risk || Mitigation ||
| Hidden vendor customisations | Incremental unpicking & mapping |
| Migration complexity | Phased rollout |
| Data gaps during transition | Dual-run validation |
| Vendor dependency | Native-only enforcement |

⸻

h2. 9. Summary

This SDD defines a product-aligned, cost-efficient, and scalable Global PIS architecture that:
	•	Reduces vendor dependency
	•	Clarifies JPM vs Tink responsibilities
	•	Enables confident contract renegotiation
	•	Positions the platform for future growth
